# From Kant to Existentialism: Modern and Contemporary Philosophy

> Kant fused rationalism and empiricism by showing the mind structures
> experience; Hegel made history itself philosophical; and the
> existentialists put the concrete, choosing individual back at the
> centre.

## 1. Kant's Copernican Revolution

**Immanuel Kant** (1724–1804) proposed asking not how our knowledge
conforms to objects (Hume's problem) but how objects conform to our
cognition: the mind supplies the forms of experience — space, time,
the categories. This explains how **synthetic a priori** judgements
are possible: informative claims that hold independently of experience.

[definition]
**Phenomena** are things as they appear, structured by our cognition.
**Noumena** are things-in-themselves — thinkable, never knowable. We
know the world as appearance, not as it is apart from knowers.
[/definition]

In ethics, Kant's **categorical imperative** commands unconditionally:

1. Act only on maxims you can will as universal law.
2. Treat humanity — in yourself and others — never merely as a means,
   but always at the same time as an end.

His essay "What Is Enlightenment?" (1784) adds *sapere aude* — dare
to know — the exit from self-incurred immaturity.

## 2. Hegel: History as Logic

**G. W. F. Hegel** (1770–1831) makes reality the self-development of
**Geist** (Spirit) coming to know itself — **absolute idealism**.
Development runs dialectically — popularly "thesis–antithesis–
synthesis"; Hegel's own terms move from the **abstract** through the
**negative** to the **concrete**. History is freedom's unfolding.

[example]
The master–slave dialectic (Phenomenology of Spirit, 1807): the
master seeks recognition from the slave, but recognition from an
unfree being is worthless — domination undermines itself. Genuine
self-consciousness requires mutual recognition between equals.
[/example]

## 3. Three Revolts Against the System

| Rebel | Revolt | Core idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Kierkegaard (1813–1855) | Against the system swallowing the individual | Truth is subjectivity; faith is a leap |
| Marx (1818–1883) | Against Spirit as the mover | Historical materialism: production drives ideas |
| Nietzsche (1844–1900) | Against all metaphysical consolations | God is dead; will to power; perspectivism |

**Kierkegaard**, the father of existentialism, insists the existing
individual outranks any system, moving through aesthetic, ethical,
and religious stages — the leap of faith beyond reason.

**Marx** turns the dialectic "right side up": the economic base shapes
law, religion, and ideas (the superstructure); history is class struggle.

**Nietzsche** diagnoses the **death of God** — the collapse of the
shared moral horizon — traces master and slave moralities, and holds
that knowing is **perspectival** and life a **will to power**.

## 4. Existentialism

**Jean-Paul Sartre** (1905–1980): **existence precedes essence** — no
fixed human nature; we are what we make of ourselves, therefore
**radically free** and responsible. Fleeing that freedom by hiding in
roles is **bad faith** (*Being and Nothingness*, 1943).

**Simone de Beauvoir** (1908–1986) applied the analysis to gender: "One
is not born, but rather becomes, a woman" (*The Second Sex*, 1949) —
femininity is a constructed situation for a situated freedom.

**Albert Camus** (1913–1960) names our condition the **absurd** — our
demand for meaning met by the universe's silence. *The Myth of
Sisyphus* (1942) answers with revolt: "One must imagine Sisyphus happy."

## 5. Phenomenology: The Method Behind It

**Edmund Husserl** (1859–1938) founded it with "to the things
themselves": describe experience as given. Its core finding is
**intentionality** — consciousness is always of something.

**Martin Heidegger** (1889–1976), in *Being and Time* (1927), asks the
question of Being: we are **Dasein**, thrown into a world of concerns,
and authenticity means facing our **being-toward-death**.

## 6. Analytic and Continental Philosophy

| | Analytic | Continental |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Style | Small arguments, formal logic, clarity | Big systems, interpretation, history |
| Homes | Britain, Austria, United States | Germany, France |
| Central figures | Frege, Russell, Wittgenstein | Hegel, Nietzsche, Heidegger, Sartre |
| Typical questions | Meaning, reference, science | Being, meaning of life, power, history |

@diagram tree id="philosophy-modern-tree" layout="tree" aria="Philosophy after Kant branching into four movements"
  "After Kant" -> "German idealism"
  "After Kant" -> Existentialism
  "After Kant" -> Phenomenology
  "After Kant" -> "Analytic philosophy"
@/diagram

Twentieth-century snapshots:

- **Ludwig Wittgenstein** (1889–1951): the later *Philosophical
  Investigations* treats meaning as use within **language games** —
  philosophy's puzzles are grammar misfiring.
- **Karl Popper** (1902–1994): a theory is scientific only if
  **falsifiable**; science grows by conjectures and refutations.
- **Thomas Kuhn** (1922–1996): science works inside a **paradigm**
  until anomalies force a revolutionary shift (1962).

## 7. Key Takeaways

| Thinker | Signature claim |
| --- | --- |
| Kant | Mind structures experience; categorical imperative |
| Hegel | Spirit develops dialectically toward freedom |
| Kierkegaard | Truth is subjectivity; the leap of faith |
| Marx | Historical materialism |
| Nietzsche | God is dead; will to power; perspectivism |
| Sartre | Existence precedes essence; radical freedom |
| de Beauvoir | One becomes a woman |
| Camus | The absurd; revolt |
| Wittgenstein | Meaning as use; language games |
| Popper and Kuhn | Falsification; paradigms |
